From 644ac537ee38eab9d626d1222f1bf861e2bda729 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dr Jonathan Richard Robert Kimmitt <jonathan@kimmitt.uk>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:52:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] map: byte lanes of a memory wider than one tile merge
 four to a block RAM

mergeLanes() put the byte lanes of a memory back into one RAMB36E1 with a
write enable per byte only when the whole memory fitted the tile's 32
data bits.  A wider memory kept one tile per lane, 8 of its 32 bits used:
a cache of 1024 x 512 bits with 64 byte enables was 64 block RAMs.  Lanes
are now grouped in order, as many to a tile as fit, so that cache is 16.
A memory that fits one tile is named as before; the tiles of a wider one
are <mem>_tile<n>.

Two things found on the way:
- the lanes' shared pins (clock, addresses, read enables) were compared
  as mapped net names, which differ from lane to lane when the address is
  logic and not a named signal, so such memories never merged, whatever
  their width (legend64's 1024 x 16 tag memory was two tiles).  They are
  compared as AIG literals now.
- with --struct-only-report, a memory in byte lanes that is not merged
  says why.

Checked on a 1024 x 128-bit memory with 16 byte enables, one read port of
its own and one at the write address: 4 RAMB36E1, and the mapped netlist
against the RTL in xsim with the unisim models, 60,000 random cycles, no
mismatch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
